## Summary

After running `$B handoff`, closing the visible Chrome window (the normal end-of-CAPTCHA flow) kills the entire browse daemon. The handoff path installs a legacy `process.exit(1)` disconnect handler that bypasses the `onDisconnect` → `shutdown()` cleanup the `connect`-to-real-Chrome path was upgraded to use back in the cleanup-on-disconnect refactor (CHANGELOG entry "Closing the headed browser window now cleans up properly").

From the user's perspective the browser "just crashes" — both the visible window and the daemon disappear together, the sidebar-agent is left dangling, and any session state is lost.

## Repro

1. Start the daemon in headless mode (anything that calls `$B goto` works).
2. `$B handoff "test"` — visible Chrome opens at the current URL.
3. Click the red close button on the Chrome window (or solve the CAPTCHA and close it — same result).
4. Any subsequent `$B` command auto-restarts the daemon fresh in `Mode: launched` — session lost.

No Chromium crash dump lands in `~/Library/Logs/DiagnosticReports/` because Chromium itself is exiting cleanly — it's the Node process exiting hard via `process.exit(1)`.

## Root cause

`browse/src/browser-manager.ts:1336-1342`, inside `handoff()`:

```ts
// Register crash handler on new browser
if (this.browser) {
  this.browser.on('disconnected', () => {
    if (this.intentionalDisconnect) return;
    console.error('[browse] FATAL: Chromium process crashed or was killed. Server exiting.');
    process.exit(1);
  });
}
```

Compare the equivalent handler in `launchHeaded()` at `browse/src/browser-manager.ts:550-571`, which has the correct shape:

```ts
if (this.browser) {
  this.browser.on('disconnected', () => {
    if (this.intentionalDisconnect) return;
    console.error('[browse] Real browser disconnected (user closed or crashed).');
    console.error('[browse] Run `$B connect` to reconnect.');
    if (!this.onDisconnect) {
      process.exit(2);
      return;
    }
    try {
      const result = this.onDisconnect();
      if (result && typeof (result as Promise<void>).catch === 'function') {
        (result as Promise<void>).catch((err) => {
          console.error('[browse] onDisconnect rejected:', err);
          process.exit(2);
        });
      }
    } catch (err) {
      console.error('[browse] onDisconnect threw:', err);
      process.exit(2);
    }
  });
}
```

Both paths use `chromium.launchPersistentContext()` against the same `~/.gstack/chromium-profile` user-data-dir, so they need the same cleanup contract. The handoff path was missed when the connect path was upgraded.

The downstream effects the v1.18-era CHANGELOG entry called out — stale sidebar-agent processes, unsaved chat session, leftover profile locks, stale `browse.json` — all apply to the handoff close path today.

## Proposed fix

Replace the handler in `handoff()` with the same `onDisconnect`-callback shape `launchHeaded()` uses. I have this patched and rebuilt locally; happy to send a PR if useful. The diff is small (~25 lines added, the old handler block replaced).

