## What happens

`ensureStateDir()` appends `.gstack/` to the git root's `.gitignore` on every browse-server start where that file doesn't already contain the literal line. It decides whether to append by reading the file's text, so if you already ignore `.gstack/` through your global `core.excludesFile`, you still get the line written into the repo's `.gitignore`. Revert it and the next daemon start puts it back.

The damage shows up on shared repos. Mine is a Jetpack checkout where `.gitignore` is tracked and team-owned, so every `/browse`, `/qa`, `/design-review`, `/canary`, or `/scrape` leaves a modified tracked file sitting in `git status` — one `git add -A` away from riding along in a PR that has nothing to do with gstack.

## Where

`browse/src/config.ts`, in `ensureStateDir()`. Same on main as of 1.60.1.0:

```js
const content = fs.readFileSync(gitignorePath, 'utf-8');
if (!content.match(/^\.gstack\/?$/m)) {
  const separator = content.endsWith('\n') ? '' : '\n';
  fs.appendFileSync(gitignorePath, `${separator}.gstack/\n`);
}
```

That text match is the entire test. Git is never asked whether the path is already ignored, so `core.excludesFile`, `.git/info/exclude`, and any parent `.gitignore` are all invisible to it.

Callers: `cli.ts` (two sites) and `server.ts` (two sites), so it fires on any daemon start.

## Repro

```sh
# .gstack/ is already in ~/.config/git/ignore
mkdir repro && cd repro && git init -q
printf 'node_modules/\n' > .gitignore
git add .gitignore && git commit -qm init

git check-ignore -v .gstack/
# ~/.config/git/ignore:8:.gstack/   .gstack/     <- already ignored

bun -e 'import {resolveConfig,ensureStateDir} from "<gstack>/browse/src/config.ts"; ensureStateDir(resolveConfig(process.env));'

cat .gitignore
# node_modules/
# .gstack/     <- appended anyway

git status --porcelain
#  M .gitignore
```

Reverting `.gitignore` and re-running reproduces it every time. It is idempotent within a checkout, so it doesn't stack duplicates; it just comes back after any revert.

## Suggested fix

Ask git instead of reading the file, and bail early when the answer is yes:

```js
export function isIgnoredByGit(projectDir: string, relPath: string): boolean {
  try {
    const proc = Bun.spawnSync(['git', 'check-ignore', '-q', '--', relPath], {
      cwd: projectDir, stdout: 'pipe', stderr: 'pipe', timeout: 2_000,
    });
    // 0 = ignored, 1 = not ignored, 128 = error (not a repo, bad pathspec)
    return proc.exitCode === 0;
  } catch {
    return false;
  }
}
```

then in `ensureStateDir()`, after the state dir is created:

```js
if (isIgnoredByGit(config.projectDir, '.gstack/')) return;
```

Returning `false` on exit 128 keeps the current behavior for non-repos and for a broken or missing git, so the only change is "already ignored, so don't touch the file."

Regression test that fails before and passes after — it sets `core.excludesFile` inside a scratch repo rather than depending on the developer's real global config:

```js
test('leaves .gitignore alone when git already ignores .gstack/ globally', () => {
  // git init tmpDir; write .gstack/ to an excludes file; git config core.excludesFile <that file>
  // .gitignore starts as 'node_modules/\n'
  ensureStateDir(config);
  expect(fs.readFileSync(gitignorePath, 'utf-8')).toBe('node_modules/\n');
  expect(fs.existsSync(path.join(tmpDir, '.gstack'))).toBe(true);
});
```

Running this locally: 42 pass / 0 fail in `browse/test/config.test.ts` with the guard, 41 pass / 1 fail without it. The four existing `ensureStateDir` gitignore tests use non-repo temp dirs, so `check-ignore` returns 128 there and they're unaffected.

Happy to open a PR if the approach looks right.

