## Summary

`restrictDirectoryPermissions()` / `restrictFilePermissions()` in `browse/src/file-permissions.ts` pass an **unqualified** Windows username to `icacls`. On any machine where the **hostname equals the username**, the bare name does not resolve to the user account, `icacls` writes an ACE for the *machine* SID instead, and because the call also passes `/inheritance:r`, the directory ends up with a single ACE that matches no one.

Result: gstack browse locks itself out of its own `.gstack/` state directory on first run and can never start on that machine.

## Affected code

`browse/src/file-permissions.ts` (introduced in #1308):

```ts
const user = os.userInfo().username;
execFileSync(
  'icacls',
  [dirPath, '/inheritance:r', '/grant:r', `${user}:(OI)(CI)(F)`],
  { stdio: 'ignore' },
);
```

Same pattern at the file-level function.

## Reproduction

Windows 11, machine name `ESLAM`, user `ESLAM\eslam`. gstack 1.58.1.0 and 1.60.2.0 (identical code, no commits touch this file between the two).

```
$ browse status
[browse] Another instance is starting the server, waiting...
[browse] Timed out waiting for another instance to start the server
```

Resulting ACL on the state dir:

```
IdentityReference             FileSystemRights  AccessControlType
-----------------             ----------------  -----------------
S-1-5-21-<redacted>           FullControl       Allow
```

That SID is the machine SID with **no RID**. The real account is `S-1-5-21-<redacted>-1001`. Inheritance is stripped (`AreAccessRulesProtected: True`), so no other ACE remains and the owner cannot enumerate the directory it just created.

Name resolution on the affected box:

| Form | Result |
| --- | --- |
| `eslam` | `NTAccount.Translate` throws "Some or all identity references could not be translated" |
| `ESLAM\eslam` | `S-1-5-21-...-1001` |
| `whoami /user /fo csv /nh` | `S-1-5-21-...-1001` |

Reproduces with a brand-new binary against a clean state dir, so it is not stale state.

## Why it is hard to diagnose

Two things hide it:

1. `stdio: 'ignore'` plus the swallowing `catch` means the bad `icacls` invocation is never surfaced. It also does not *fail* here, it "succeeds" while writing a useless ACE.
2. `acquireServerLock()` returns `null` on **any** exception, including the `EACCES` from the bricked directory. The caller then reports "Another instance is starting the server", which points the user at a phantom concurrency problem rather than at permissions.

## Suggested fix

Resolve the current user's SID and hand `icacls` the literal `*<SID>` form, which is immune to name-resolution ambiguity. Fall back to the domain-qualified name.

```ts
let cachedSid: string | null | undefined;

function currentUserSid(): string | null {
  if (cachedSid !== undefined) return cachedSid;
  try {
    const out = execFileSync('whoami', ['/user', '/fo', 'csv', '/nh'], { encoding: 'utf8' });
    const m = out.match(/S-1-[\d-]+/);
    cachedSid = m ? m[0] : null;
  } catch {
    cachedSid = null;
  }
  return cachedSid;
}

function principal(): string {
  const sid = currentUserSid();
  if (sid) return `*${sid}`;
  const domain = process.env.USERDOMAIN || os.hostname();
  return `${domain}\\${os.userInfo().username}`;
}
```

Then use `principal()` in both `restrictFilePermissions` and `restrictDirectoryPermissions`.

Two smaller hardening suggestions worth folding in:

- Drop `stdio: 'ignore'` in favour of capturing stderr, so a bad grant is visible.
- After hardening, do a cheap `fs.accessSync(dir, fs.constants.R_OK | fs.constants.W_OK)` and warn loudly if the directory the tool just secured is no longer usable by the process that created it. That converts a silent brick into an actionable message.

## Workaround for affected users

Additive grant using the SID form. Survives the daemon re-applying its own hardening on every start, because `/grant:r` only replaces the entry for the principal it names.

```
icacls "<repo>\.gstack" /grant "*S-1-5-21-...-1001:(OI)(CI)(F)"
```

Confirmed working: browse started cleanly on the first attempt afterwards and has stayed up across subsequent invocations.

## Summary

`generate --toc <input.md> <output.pdf>` fails, but `generate <input.md> <output.pdf> --toc` works. The documented usage pattern in the skill's own help text (`$P generate --cover --toc essay.md essay.pdf`) triggers the bug.

## Root cause

`make-pdf/src/cli.ts`, `parseArgs()` (~lines 33-49):

```ts
for (let i = 0; i < args.length; i++) {
    const a = args[i];
    if (a.startsWith("--")) {
      const key = a.slice(2);
      const next = args[i + 1];
      if (next !== undefined && !next.startsWith("--")) {
        flags[key] = next;
        i++;
      } else {
        flags[key] = true;
      }
    } else if (!command) {
      command = a;
    } else {
      positional.push(a);
    }
}
```

This parser has no concept of boolean vs. value-taking flags. For *any* `--flag` followed by a token that doesn't start with `--`, it greedily consumes that token as the flag's value. `--toc` (and `--cover`, `--no-chapter-breaks`) are booleans with no value — but when one precedes a positional argument (e.g. `<input.md>`), the parser swallows the positional as the flag's "value", shifting everything else out of position.

## Repro

```bash
$P generate --toc input.md output.pdf
# → Error: input file not found: output.pdf
```

Because `--toc` ate `input.md` as its value, `output.pdf` became the new positional[0] (treated as input.md), and there's no positional[1] for output — the tool then reports the *output* path as a missing *input* file, which is also a confusing error message independent of the parsing bug.

## Workaround (confirmed working)

Put `--toc` after both positional args:

```bash
$P generate input.md output.pdf --toc
```

## Suggested fix

Maintain an explicit set of known boolean-only flags (`toc`, `cover`, `no-chapter-breaks`, `no-confidential`, `quiet`, `verbose`, `strict`, `allow-network`, `page-numbers`/`no-page-numbers`, `tagged`/`no-tagged`, `outline`/`no-outline`) and only consume `next` as a value for flags NOT in that set — rather than inferring "boolean vs value" from whether the next token happens to start with `--`. This also affects `--cover` and `--no-chapter-breaks` (any boolean flag), not just `--toc`.

As a smaller fix, the "input file not found" error message could also be improved to name which argument it resolved as the input, to make misparses like this easier to diagnose.

## Environment

- gstack version: 1.60.1.0
- make-pdf invoked via the Claude Code skill wrapper (`generate` subcommand)

## Summary

On Windows, every `browse` daemon opens **two visible console windows** — one for
`chrome-headless-shell.exe` and one for the `bun.exe` terminal-agent. They persist for the
life of the daemon and stack up: with 4 daemons alive I had 8 windows covering the screen.

Root cause is that Node defaults `windowsHide` to `false`, and nothing in the browse
codebase sets it. `windowsHide` appears **zero times** in the 833 KB `server-node.mjs` bundle.

## Environment

- Windows 11 Pro 26100, gstack v1.63.0.0 (`1d41ee3`)
- Node daemon path (`server-node.mjs` + `bun-polyfill.cjs`), playwright-core 1.58.2
- Windows Terminal registered as the default terminal application

## Why a window appears

The daemon is spawned detached with `stdio: 'ignore'`, so it has **no console of its own**.
Any console-subsystem child it then spawns causes Windows to allocate a fresh console. With
Windows Terminal registered as the default terminal app, console handoff surfaces that as a
real window, titled with the child's full exe path.

Two spawn sites, both missing `windowsHide`:

1. **`playwright-core/lib/server/utils/processLauncher.js`** (1.58.2) builds `spawnOptions`
   with `detached` but never `windowsHide` → `chrome-headless-shell.exe`
2. **`browse/src/bun-polyfill.cjs`** — `Bun.spawn` / `Bun.spawnSync` pass `stdio`, `env`,
   `cwd`, `timeout`, but not `windowsHide` → `bun.exe` running `terminal-agent.ts`

## Why one fix covers both

Patching vendored playwright is fragile, and `chromium.launch()` exposes no `windowsHide`
option. But esbuild's `__toESM` — used at `processLauncher.js:38`,
`var childProcess = __toESM(require("child_process"))` — copies module exports as **live
getters** (`__defProp(to, key, { get: () => from[key] })`). So `childProcess.spawn` resolves
through to the `child_process` exports object at *call* time, and patching that object inside
`bun-polyfill.cjs` reaches Playwright even though it was imported earlier.

`bun-polyfill.cjs` is already the Windows compatibility layer and is required at the top of
the bundle, which makes it the natural home for this.

## Patch

Against `browse/src/bun-polyfill.cjs`. Sets `windowsHide: true` on the polyfill's own two
spawns, and wraps the `child_process` exports with a `windowsHide` default. Guarded to
`win32`, and an explicit caller-supplied `windowsHide` is never overridden.

Worth noting: `windowsHide` only suppresses the **console** window. GUI windows are
unaffected, so `browse --headed` still shows a real browser — verified.

